menu "EspDrop"

config ESPDROP_BRIDGE_MODE
    bool "Bridge mode (USB host-link instead of interactive console)"
    default n
    help
        When enabled, the firmware starts the COBS-framed USB host-link bridge
        (see bridge.c / the M2 data-path plan) instead of the interactive serial
        console. Use this build to run the host-side awdl0 bridge and the M2.0
        echo test. Leave it off for the M0/M1 console workflow (tx/rx/sync/...).

config ESPDROP_AWDL_NAME
    string "AWDL host name we advertise"
    default "espdrop-c5" if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C5
    default "espdrop-c3"
    help
        Name carried in our AWDL frames (ARPA TLV). Shows up in sniffer captures
        and in the peer lists of other devices; it is not the AirDrop name.

config ESPDROP_AWDL_CHANNEL
    int "AWDL channel at boot"
    range 1 177
    default 6
    help
        Channel the AWDL node starts on. 6 is the 2.4 GHz social channel and the
        only choice on the ESP32-C3. A 5 GHz chip (ESP32-C5) also accepts 44 or
        149 for single-channel experiments. Both the console and the bridge can
        change it at runtime with `chan`.

config ESPDROP_WIFI_COUNTRY
    string "Wi-Fi country code (empty = driver default)"
    default "DE" if SOC_WIFI_SUPPORT_5G
    default ""
    help
        Two-letter country code passed to esp_wifi_set_country_code() at start.
        Empty keeps the driver default ("01", world-safe). On a 5 GHz chip a real
        country fixes which 5 GHz channels and power limits the driver applies.
        Independently of this, the firmware caps the TX power on channels 149
        and up (EU short-range-device band, 25 mW EIRP).

endmenu
